ribe the fixed state, so the docs-authoring epic runs after the Dashboard remediation is complete. Per the Step 4 docs-authoring priority exception, this edge does not push E002's P2 priority onto E001 — E001's priority comes from HLR-1/HLR-2 (P1). |\n39\t\n40\tNo other edges: there is exactly one code epic and one docs epic; no Investigation epics are needed (the strategy's open questions table is empty and the audit report enumerates the exact defects per flow — no unknown changes which epics exist or what they do).\n41\t\n42\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n43\t\n44\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n45\t|-----|----------|-------------|---------|-------|\n46\t| HLR-1 | P1 | Four audited core flows fully operable by keyboard, no focus traps or unreachable controls | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Core scope of the Dashboard remediation epic; audit report enumerates the defects. |\n47\t| HLR-2 | P1 | Visible focus indicators consistent across the audited flows | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Same epic as HLR-1: the focus-indicator consistency work edits the same controls, modals, and flows as the keyboard-operability fixes — one mechanical pass over the same four surfaces. Same priority (P1), so no bundling tension. |\n48\t| HLR-3 | P2 | Conformance documentation (VPAT and accessibility statement) updated and republished with technical review | RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Separate work stream: substantive content authoring with a distinct review cycle (Red Hat docs content process), per Step 5 Rule 1 work-product-type boundary. Priority P2 per the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter priority: Normal → P2). |\n49\t\n50\tAll three HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n51\t\n52\t## Health Warnings\n53\t\n54\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy omits the Impacted Teams table, the Dependencies table, Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and Non-Functional Requirements. Acceptance criteria for both epics were derived from the HLRs and problem statement; team assignments were taken from architecture context (Dashboard) and assumed (Documentation — see below). Human verification of these derivations is recommended.\n55\t- **Team assumption for Documentation:** the strategy names no owning team for the Documentation component. \"RHOAI Documentation\" is used as a placeholder assumption; correct it at planning time if the docs work is owned elsewhere.\n56\t- **Planning note (epic size):** the Dashboard remediation epic covers four audited flows (project creation, workbench spawn, model deployment, pipeline run submission). The audit report is stated to enumerate the exact defects per flow, so this is one logical change — a single mechanical accessibility pass over the same surfaces, where a split would fragment the fix pattern and duplicate the audit walk-through. If the defect list turns out to exceed roughly a sprint of effort, re-split by flow. **Re-split trigger:** re-split this epic by audited flow if the audit report's enumerated defects exceed the ~2-week guideline when triaged.\n57\t\n58\t## Tiered Delivery\n59\t\n60\tNot applicable — no external dependencies with uncertain timing; all work is internal to the Dashboard and Documentation components.\n61\t\n62\t## Component Mapping Notes\n63\t\n64\t- Strategy \"Affected Components: Dashboard\" maps verbatim to the canonical component `Dashboard` (`.context/rhai-components.txt`); owning team taken from `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md` (RHOAI Dashboard).\n65\t- Strategy \"Affected Components: Documentation\" maps verbatim to the canonical component `Documentation`. No architecture-context file exists for Documentation, so the team could not be corroborated from context (see Health Warnings).\n66\t- Dashboard is Konflux-onboarded per architecture context, and both components already exist in the platform — no onboarding chain (Rule 20) is needed.\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-814155bc85e696fc","agen